{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 38, :comments ["<b>Which HaShem commanded Moses on Mount Sinai.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted that all the commandments were stated to Moses on Mount Sinai — their general principles, their specific details, and their minutiae. And so the commandments of the book of Leviticus were given on Mount Sinai." "<b>And indeed,</b> by way of the plain sense (peshat), what is meant by his saying 'on Mount Sinai' and then going back and mentioning 'in the wilderness of Sinai': the meaning of the verse is — 'this is the Torah for the burnt offering and for the sacrifices that HaShem commanded on Mount Sinai, on the day He commanded them in the wilderness.' After saying 'on Mount Sinai,' he mentioned 'in the wilderness of Sinai' so that you should not understand from this that he was literally on the mountain, in the place of glory where He spoke with him the Ten Commandments; and He also needed to mention 'in the wilderness of Sinai' so that you should not understand 'in the wilderness of Sinai' to mean after they had journeyed from before the mountain. And because of this the verse mentioned both of them: 'on Mount Sinai, in the wilderness of Sinai.' And the sense of 'on (ב) Mount Sinai' is 'before (לפני) Mount Sinai,' and the verse mentioned that this was in the wilderness of Sinai, before the mountain, within its boundary and close to it, and there was the Tent of Meeting — as he said at the beginning of the matter (Leviticus 1:1): ״ויקרא אל משה וידבר ה׳ אליו מאהל מועד לאמר״. He now informed us of the place of the Tent of Meeting. And all that the verse says — 'a perpetual burnt offering made on Mount Sinai' — its meaning is 'before Mount Sinai,' for on the mountain itself they did not offer. Thus wrote the Ramban, of blessed memory." "<b>On the day He commanded the children of Israel to offer their sacrifices to HaShem in the wilderness of Sinai.</b> See, here it explicitly explains that the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded concerning the sacrifices. If so, one may ask about what the prophet said (Jeremiah 7:22): ״כי לא דברתי את אבותיכם ולא צויתים״ etc. ״על דברי עולה וזבח.״ But the matter is thus: certainly the verse is what commanded concerning the sacrifices, and this was among His abundant mercies, because flesh and blood is prone to sin, and no person is saved from it, not even the righteous — as it is written (Ecclesiastes 7:20): ״כי אדם אין צדיק בארץ אשר יעשה טוב ולא יחטא״. Therefore the commandment of sacrifices came from Him, may He be exalted, to serve before Him as atonement for our iniquities, so that Elohim would be propitiated toward us and we would not perish. But His will is that we obey His voice and not sin, and not need sacrifices; rather, that we bring sacrifices and not obey His voice — this He did not command. And concerning this the prophet said: ״ולא צויתים על דברי עולה וזבח.״ And we find explicitly in the verse (1 Samuel 15:22): ״הנה שמוע מזבח טוב,״ and a full verse says, David, of blessed memory, (Psalm 40:7): ״זבח ומנחה לא חפצת״ — but what do You desire? That we obey Your voice. This is what he said (there): ״אזנים כרית לי עולה וחטאה לא שאלת.״ For it is well known that the essence of the sacrifices is obeying the voice of HaShem, may He be exalted, and from this he immediately mentions (Jeremiah 7:23): ״כי אם את הדבר הזה צויתי אותם לאמר שמעו בקולי.״ And if the owner of the sacrifice obeys the voice of HaShem, may He be exalted, then his sacrifice is accepted and favorably received — as it is written (Psalm 4:5): ״רגזו ואל תחטאו אמרו בלבבכם״, and immediately after: ״זבחו זבחי צדק״, and its meaning is: be willing to sacrifice sacrifices of righteousness, that you not sin. And if he does not obey the voice of HaShem, may He be exalted, his sacrifice is not accepted but is called 'the sacrifice of the wicked,' and the verse says (Proverbs 15:8): ״זבח רשעים תועבת ה׳ ותפלת ישרים רצונו״ — the meaning of the verse being that the prayer of the righteous is good and more acceptable than the sacrifice of the wicked. And so Solomon, of blessed memory, said (Ecclesiastes 4:17): ״וקרוב לשמוע מתת הכסילים זבח״ — he says: HaShem is near to hear the prayer of the righteous and desires it more than He would desire the sacrifice of fools. And on this theme Isaiah, of blessed memory, spoke concerning the wicked who offer a sacrifice while in their wickedness — that it is accounted to them as a great transgression; this is what he said (Isaiah 66:3): ״שוחט השור מכה איש זובח השה עורף כלב מעלה מנחה דם חזיר״ etc. — he explains that the wicked man who slaughters the ox to offer it as a sacrifice is like one who slays a person, and one who slaughters the lamb to offer it is as though he were breaking a dog's neck to offer it before Him, and one who brings a meal offering is as though he were offering swine's blood before Him." "<b>Now see:</b> the dog and the swine are both unclean, but each of them has a sign of purity — for the dog splits the hoof, and the swine splits the hoof and is fully cloven. Therefore he likened the wicked to them, for they show in themselves that there is in them some good deed in their offering a sacrifice, yet behold, they are unclean in evil deeds. And this is the meaning of the verse: ״כי לא דברתי את אבותיכם ולא צויתים על דברי עולה וזבח״ — that is to say, without their obeying My voice."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:24:07.194524620Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}